    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I would categorize those texts under a section titled \"Traditional Economic Perspectives on Wealth Accumulation.\" In the syllabus, I would clearly state that these readings are included to provide a comprehensive understanding of the various economic theories and models that have been proposed to explain wealth accumulation. However, I would also emphasize that these perspectives are not without their limitations and that they often fail to account for the systemic barriers faced by marginalized communities. I would encourage students to critically analyze these texts and consider how they may perpetuate a narrow view of economic success that overlooks the impact of structural inequality. By presenting these texts alongside critiques of the individual-choice framework, I would aim to foster a more nuanced understanding of the complex factors that contribute to racial wealth gaps. It's important to acknowledge that while traditional economic theories may offer valuable insights, they are not sufficient to fully explain the systemic racism that underlies these disparities. We must also consider the sociological critiques that challenge the assumption that individual agency alone can overcome the structural barriers that perpetuate inequality. By presenting a balanced view of the various perspectives, I hope to encourage students to critically engage with the material and develop a more comprehensive understanding of the factors that contribute to racial wealth gaps.",
